  "statement": "Treating software code as an unpredictable autonomous force demanding exotic legal categories ignores how technology is actually manufactured, deployed, and monetized by corporate actors. Companies build models using vast clusters of physical infrastructure, consuming millions of gallons of water in places like The Dalles, Oregon, while keeping human laborers in Nairobi at two dollars an hour to filter toxic training data. The resulting outputs are not mysterious acts of God or alien intelligence. They are engineered commercial products subject to standard failures of quality control and corporate cost-cutting.\n\nMandatory safety architectures and pre-market compliance audits do not protect the public from algorithmic harm. They function as regulatory moats that price out independent open-source developers while shielding dominant firms from civil liability. Large technology companies spend millions lobbying for complex certification frameworks precisely because smaller competitors cannot afford the compliance overhead. Meanwhile, actual injured parties receive no recourse through these top-down administrative checkboxes.\n\nDownstream operational monitoring and strict product liability under established tort law remain the only effective remedies for algorithmic failures. If a deployment causes financial loss, privacy breaches, or physical injury, the entity that profited from the deployment must bear the financial cost. Shifting the legal standard to recognize unmitigated model deployment as a design defect cuts through compliance theater and forces corporations to internalize the actual physical and economic costs of their systems.",
