  "statement": "Relying on retroactive Federal Trade Commission enforcement to manage AI deployment failures is a dangerous illusion that ignores the reality of unrecoverable harm. The Boeing 737 MAX killed 346 people because the Federal Aviation Administration delegated safety certification to the manufacturer and relied on post-market penalties rather than pre-market verification. Algorithmic disgorgement orders issued by regulators months after a model launch cannot restore lost human lives, reverse systemic financial ruin, or un-leak compromised biometric databases. Post-market punishment punishes corporations after catastrophe strikes while offering zero structural protection to the public ex ante.\n\nComplex adaptive systems require rigorous pre-deployment safety boundaries because probabilistic software failures defy reactive remediation. The artificial intelligence industry is projected to be worth $244 billion, yet deployers demand exemptions from the baseline safety standards applied to civil aviation, medical devices, and civil engineering. When an unaligned model is deployed into feedback-contaminated environments without pre-market verification, corporate insolvency caps financial liability long before societal damages are fully paid. Enforcement agencies lack the technical capacity and staffing to audit hundreds of billions of parameters in real time once a system is already operating at scale.\n\nMandatory pre-deployment testing and independent safety cases are the non-negotiable price of public safety in high-consequence domains. More than 5,000 new AI startups in the United States were funded between 2013 and 2023, each racing to bypass verification in the name of deployment velocity. Congress enacted the Algorithmic Accountability Act to establish proactive oversight precisely because voluntary compliance and retroactive litigation failed to prevent algorithmic bias in healthcare systems that diverted care from vulnerable populations. Society cannot trade irreversible structural stability for the commercial convenience of moving fast and breaking systems.",
